Hi Simon great work !

Ok to answer (others will also)

Drying;

You said drying horizontal? You meant vertical, like leaning angled on a wall. The more smooth the drying process the less ripples and color variations, always dry my plates with the emulsion facing out. Hopefully others that know more about this will post.

Stray edge light;

Put a thin black tape along leading and trailing edge of plate to block this reflection, or you may choose to use black opaqe marker used in overhead projectors , your teachers proably don’t have any on hand but the local business product store should have a pen or two, just be sure to wipe it off with a mois towel (water soluable).

Backround;

Yes a picture will work as long as its stable. A method used it to laminate a photo to a piece of glass, be sure to use a B&W photo with the contrast as high a possible to provide a bright image …………….

All good words you guys, Simon listen to what we all are saying. 

If i were you this is what i would do;

Dont change the new and larger size of the spot, take your new film and cut it into four pieces and now switch back to the jig you used sucessfuly prior, now try and duplicate your previous good holograms. If it works you know the film is good and your exposuer time is correct, then move on to shooting 4x5s. As we all pretty much stated ,"THE PINS ARE NO GOOD,THE HOT glue IS NO GOOD (use epoxy to glue the RODS and not thin pins). now you may think we are all full of hot air but you are on a time line and its running out fast (so will the film). So Simon PLEASE get rid of the pins and epoxy some rods in there place. Its better to see a 3 little rods holding your plate , then to see nothing at all !
